notice is  that this api returns an option of a  game box so  this api is really supposed to give us  um  the box at the position if there is one  or nothing if there is no box at the  position and the same with the spot  and you might be wondering um  about options like why we chose to use  the the option type and really um  what what options are and why they are  so common in in rust so let's talk a bit  about options so  um as i mentioned so far options are  basically you either have something with  a value or you have nothing so you can  see on the left hand side um i just took  the the definition of the enum uh for  options straight from the standard  library so it's a new enum it has two  variants some with a t or none uh which  basically as i said means you can either  have something and you'll get the actual  value inside that or you could have  nothing um  options are very common in ros code um  i haven't encountered them that much in  other languages um  but they do exist as a concept and the  concept that you might be familiar with  from other languages is null options  really are a typesafe way of dealing  with nullability of the having something  versus having nothing so while in a  different language you might pass a  pointer to something  and you would rely on checking if that  pointer is null or not before actually  using the the value if there is a value  in in rust you you will use options and  you will rely on the  type safety that the variants of option  give you um this has a bunch of  advantages mainly the fact that the  client code  needs to very explicitly handle both  cases both when you have something and  you have nothing and there's no  risk of really forgetting to check uh  you could of course still do the the  wrong thing uh rust will not prevent you  from doing that but it does give you an  extra layer of type safety  the other thing is that it really helps  us  express intent when we define our  function function definitions  so as yo