nd way of  writing what we wrote in the previous  option but you can see it's less verbose  we're basically just saying if we have  something in this box assign it to this  variable  return true from this condition  and move on so if we go to the next line  uh you'll see that we can do the same  for the spot and  finally now that we have both the box  and a spot  we will check the colors and we will uh  return that  similarly if  some of the options if if one of the  options was none  the iflat condition will actually return  false  so we will jump straight to line seven  cool  you might be thinking well this is  already less verbose which i completely  agree with um it's also more idiomatic  we're basically using a language  construct to deal with the options and  the if let will do the  checking of do we have something do we  have nothing and we'll basically assign  the something to a variable without us  having to check is none is some and and  do the unwrapping ourselves and this is  important because in the previous  example we could very easily forget to  check is some before we actually unwrap  the option and what that means is that  we could be unwrapping an option that is  actually none and that is a problem  because we will get a panic we will not  be able to recover from from that error  so  this code is slightly better from from  that point of view because it gives us a  bit of additional safety  the only inconvenience is the nesting  but i don't think that's such a big  problem especially when we only have two  options  um  next up let's think of a different way  that we might be able to write this code  so the other thing that we could use is  we could use a match construct uh match  again exists in other languages it's a  bit like a switch statement but a little  bit more  [Music]  elaborate you and more powerful you'll  be able to do a lot more things with the  match they will be able to do with  switch in other languages  we won't see their full power here  because this is a very simple example  but  you should know that so again we're  calling map get box we're calling map  get spot remember those return options  and  then we'll basically be able to say well  if the pattern that we've put in the  match  uh is some with a box and some with a  spot then do this  and we'll basically check the colors of  of the spot in the boxes  if  if not if  the pattern is anything else so that  could be we don't have a box or uh we  don't have a box or a spot then we'll  we'll go into line six and we'll  basically return false um as i mentioned  this is  much gives us the ability to do  more powerful things um we haven't seen  the full power of it because this is a  very simple example but i think  i think you get the point  arguably this is a little bit harder to  read we have some  slight complications in the syntax  because we're actually using a tuple to  be able to match on both sides otherwise  we would have to match on each option  individually which we could also do but  that would be a little bit longer uh  hopefully that made sense that was three  different concepts uh we talked about  options we talked about if led and we  talked about matches  hopefully that is enough to get you  interested in these concepts if if you  were not familiar with them before or if  you were uh hopefully it gives you the  appetite to dig a little bit deeper and  understand them a bit uh  deeper in terms of both how they are  implemented and how they help us write  more idiomatic rust um finally if this  sparked your interest in  making a gaming rust or getting started  with rust at all  i have a short online book that will  teach you how to make a game like  sogoban exactly what i showed you in in  the first gif um you'll basically be  able to build that at the end of this  book it's very short  i highly recommend you you try it out  and finally i put some links to my  social media profiles and  a link to the playground that includes  the code in this talk thank

